<ArticleLayout slug="all-in-cost-of-ai-video" ctaLabel="Get a market range for your video program" faq={faq}>
  <p class="standfirst">The cheap figures circulating for AI video are not lies. They are one line of a thirteen-line bill, published by people who sell that line. Here is the rest of it, including the number nobody has measured.</p>

  <h2>How much does an AI video ad cost?</h2>

  <p>Generation costs between roughly $1.50 and $18.00 for thirty finished seconds at published list rates, if nothing is retried. Nothing is ever not retried, and the retry rate is the number nobody publishes.</p>

  <p>That is the shape of this whole subject. One line of the bill is precisely knowable because two surfaces publish it. The line that swings the total by more than an order of magnitude has no benchmark anywhere, and most of the remaining lines have no published price at all.</p>

  <h2>What generation actually costs</h2>

  <p>Read off live surfaces, one at a time.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Published list prices put a second of generated video between roughly $0.05 and $0.60, depending on model tier and resolution, with audio included at the default on one of the two surfaces.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published generation price surfaces in this category, read directly on 10 September 2026, one credit card and one API price list, quoted per second of generated video, no vendor named.</p>

  <p>Credits complicate that, so it is worth converting them.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">On the credit route the same second works out at about $0.10 to $0.23 once the monthly price is divided by the monthly credit allowance and multiplied by the card's stated 12 credits a second.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: one published credit card in this category, read directly on 10 September 2026, arithmetic shown on the page, single card.</p>

  <p>Multiply either band out and thirty seconds looks trivially cheap.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Thirty seconds of finished screen time, generated once with nothing retried, costs between about $1.50 and $18.00 in generation at published list rates.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: arithmetic on published generation prices in this category, 10 September 2026, shown step by step on this page.</p>

  <p>Now the detail that makes those numbers behave differently in practice.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Neither published price surface says whether a rejected or unusable generation is billed. Both bill the attempt, not the result.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the same two published price surfaces, read in full on 10 September 2026. The absence was confirmed at source, not inferred.</p>

  <p>The attempt, not the result. Every generation you throw away is on the invoice, which means the published per-second price is not a price per finished second. It is a price per try.</p>

  <h2>How many generations does one usable shot take?</h2>

  <p>Nobody knows, and this is the load-bearing gap in every cost estimate you will read, including the cheap ones and the expensive ones.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">No independent benchmark of generations per usable shot exists. The only rate published by anyone selling generation tools comes from that seller's own unnamed internal case work, and the only figure from a named practitioner covers a single 2024 production. The two differ by roughly seventy-five times.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: five buyer queries run in English and Chinese on 10 September 2026, top ten results assessed for each. Four of the top eight on the direct question measure detection of generated video, which is a different quantity on a different population.</p>

  <p>Seventy-five times apart. That is not a range, it is two unrelated numbers, and the honest thing to do with them is show what each does to the bill rather than average them into a figure that describes nothing.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Take the same 30 seconds at four generations for each usable one and the generation bill runs about $6 to $72. Take it at the roughly 300 to 1 ratio one documented production disclosed and the same 30 seconds runs about $450 to $5,400.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: arithmetic on published generation prices in this category, 10 September 2026, applied to the two retry ratios that have been published anywhere, both shown with their limits on this page.</p>

  <p>Look at what moves the total. Price per second varies about twelvefold across every published rate. The retry rate, on the only two figures anyone has put in public, varies about seventy-fivefold. The variable everybody quotes is the smaller of the two.</p>

  <p>The higher figure deserves its context, because a single production is not a market.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">On one documented AI-assisted short film, the post-production lead put the ratio of generated source material to finished footage at about 300 to 1, with hundreds of generations of 10 to 20 seconds each behind roughly 90 seconds of finished film.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: a visual-effects trade publication's interview with the production team, April 2024. One production, the practitioner's own estimate, no counting log published, and the model generation is dated.</p>

  <p>One production, a practitioner's estimate rather than a log, and models have moved since. Treat it as the top of a plausible span rather than as a measurement. What it does establish is that ratios in the hundreds are achievable in real work, which is enough to make a four-to-one planning assumption look optimistic.</p>

  <p>That production also shows what generation does not finish.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">That same production still needed rotoscoping, artifact removal, retiming of clips that had rendered in unintended slow motion, manual cropping to create camera moves, color grading and an added grain treatment, and it ran at 480p before an external upscale.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the same trade-publication interview, April 2024, the team's own account of its post pipeline.</p>

  <h2>The line the retry rate quietly creates</h2>

  <p>Retries cost more than their generation fee, and the extra cost lands in a place no price surface touches. Somebody has to watch them.</p>

  <p>Every generated attempt has to be viewed, judged and either kept or discarded, and that is a person's time at a person's hourly rate. The arithmetic runs the opposite way from the generation bill. At four generations per usable shot, a thirty-second film means reviewing a couple of minutes of material to keep thirty seconds, which is trivial. At the higher published ratio it means sitting through a great deal of footage to keep the same thirty seconds, and every one of those attempts needs a human decision.</p>

  <p>So the two ratios do not just change the generation line by seventy-five times. They change the shape of the job. One is a production task with a small review step attached. The other is mostly a review task with generation attached, and it is staffed, scheduled and budgeted differently.</p>

  <p>This is why the generation price being close to free is less reassuring than it sounds. Cheap attempts remove the friction that used to limit how many attempts anyone made, and the cost migrates to the reviewing seat, which is not cheap and does not scale by spending more on compute. No published source measures how much time that takes, so this page puts no number on it. It belongs on your estimate as a line with your own hours in it.</p>

  <h2>Is AI video really a dollar per ad?</h2>

  <p>On generation alone, roughly yes. That concession matters, because the counter argument is not that the number is invented.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The circulating figure of roughly one to five dollars an AI ad is a market claim. No publisher in that chain states a sample, an instrument or a definition of a finished ad, and every one of them sells generation.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: a circulating market claim in this category, observed again in September 2026. Quoted as a claim, never as evidence.</p>

  <p>Run it yourself. At the cheapest published rate, a fifteen-second cut generated once is well under a dollar. So the arithmetic holds, on one line, under assumptions the claim does not state: no retries, no direction, no sound design, no clearance, no versioning, and a definition of "an ad" that means a file rather than something cleared to run.</p>

  <p>The useful comparison is against a person's time rather than against another tool's price. In the United States, published medians and the published compensation load give an hourly figure for the roles that finish this work.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">In the United States, the median annual wage was $75,420 for film and video editors, $74,990 for camera operators in television, video and film, and $114,850 for art directors. Loaded at the published private-industry compensation multiplier of about 1.43 and divided by a 2,080-hour year, that is about $52, $52 and $79 an hour.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the national occupational employment and wage statistics program, May 2025 reference period, a full establishment survey, with the load taken from the national employer compensation cost series, March 2026 reference period. US data, and the 2,080-hour year is a stated convention.</p>

  <p>At the cheapest published rate, two full minutes of generation costs less than a sixth of one loaded editor hour, on US figures. Generation is genuinely close to free next to the people around it. Which is exactly why pricing the whole film on the generation line is the wrong instrument: you are optimizing the cheapest input while the expensive ones stay unexamined.</p>

