  <p>Two rows out of thirteen. Any figure presented as the cost of an AI video was built by filling in eleven blanks from somewhere, and the page giving you that figure is usually selling one of the two rows that are real.</p>

  <h2>What AI video generation does not cover</h2>

  <p>Rights, most consistently, and the industry's own paperwork demonstrates it.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The standard bid form of the US commercial production trade body itemizes a production across sections A through P, from prep crew to breakout talent, and carries no line at all for media usage payments.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the trade body's own free bid form, updated January 2023, downloaded and read in full September 2026.</p>

  <p>Sixteen sections covering everything from prep to talent, and usage is not one of them. What you pay to keep the film running sits outside the document the industry uses to price making it, which means it sits outside any generation quote too.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The same trade body's 2026 bidding guide tells bidders to establish whether a stated budget is all in, and whether stills, social elements and behind the scenes carry separate decks and separate budgets.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the trade body's published bidding reference guide, 2026 edition.</p>

  <p>Versioning has its own trap, and it is a rights trap rather than a production one.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Under the US performers' union commercials agreement effective 1 April 2025, alternate scenes shot for one commercial in the same session are not treated as new commercials, while an edit or lift outside the defined parameters creates a new commercial and new fees.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the union commercials agreement effective April 2025, as read by two independent law firms. The union's own rate documents are not machine readable.</p>

  <p>A cutdown that steps outside those parameters is a new commercial with new fees attached. Since cutdowns are precisely what volume video programs produce, this is the line most likely to be discovered late, and generation cost has nothing to do with it.</p>

  <h2>When is AI video genuinely cheaper?</h2>

  <p>Often, and the conditions are specific enough to check against your own brief.</p>

  <p><strong>When the shot would have required something expensive to stage.</strong> A location, a crowd, a weather condition, an impossible camera move. Here generation replaces a real production line item, and the saving is the whole line rather than a rate difference.</p>

  <p><strong>When the output is a variant of something already made.</strong> Recuts, aspect ratios, background swaps and language versions built off an approved master. The direction is already paid for, so generation is doing arithmetic rather than authorship.</p>

  <p><strong>When volume is the requirement and the bar is fitness rather than craft.</strong> Performance testing, where the point is to run many variants and let the data choose.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Nearly two thirds of brands expect to increase social and short-form video production.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: global advertiser trade body content production research, March 2025, a survey of 50 global brands across more than ten sectors.</p>

  <p>And where it is not cheaper: anything whose value is the direction. Generation prices the making of frames. It does not price deciding what the frames should be, and on a brand film that decision is most of the cost and all of the risk.</p>

