  <p>The <a href="/solutions/ai-production/video">AI video production</a> page covers how generation and capture run in one pipeline, the <a href="/services/design/video-production">video production service page</a> covers the direction and finishing lines that dominate this table, and the <a href="/resources/production-cost">production cost estimator</a> will return a market range against your own volume rather than a quote.</p>

  <h2>How this page was built</h2>

  <p>Every generation price was read directly from a live published price surface on 10 September 2026, one surface at a time, and is quoted per second of generated video as that surface states it. Bands built from two independent surfaces are given as ranges. Arithmetic is shown in full so you can rerun it on your own rates. Where no figure exists, this page says so and prints nothing in its place.</p>

  <p>One thing this page deliberately does not do is give you a single percentage for generation as a share of total cost. The numerator is publishable and the denominator is not, so any such percentage would be an invention wearing a decimal point.</p>

  <p>Reviewed September 2026.</p>

  <h2>Questions buyers ask about AI video cost</h2>

  <h3>How much does an AI video ad cost?</h3>

  <p>Generation for thirty finished seconds runs about $1.50 to $18.00 at published list rates with nothing retried, and about $6 to $72 at four generations per usable shot. Everything else on a thirteen-line bill, including direction, cleanup, sound, clearance and versioning, has no published price, so no trustworthy all-in figure exists.</p>

  <h3>Is AI video really a dollar per ad?</h3>

  <p>On the generation line alone, roughly yes, and that concession is fair. The circulating one-to-five-dollar claim states no sample, no instrument and no definition of a finished ad, and every publisher in that chain sells generation. It assumes no retries, no direction, no sound, no clearance and no versioning.</p>

  <h3>What does AI video generation not cover?</h3>

  <p>Direction, shot plates, selection time, rotoscoping and artifact cleanup, retiming, upscaling, grade, sound, rights clearance and versioning. Usage rights in particular sit outside the industry's own standard bid form, which itemizes a production across sections A through P and carries no line for media usage payments at all.</p>

  <h3>How many generations does one usable shot take?</h3>

  <p>No independent benchmark exists. The only two published figures are a generation seller's own unnamed internal case work and one named practitioner's estimate of roughly 300 to 1 on a single 2024 production. They differ by about seventy-five times, which is why this page shows the bill at both rather than averaging them.</p>

  <h3>When is AI video genuinely cheaper?</h3>

  <p>When the shot would have needed an expensive location, crowd or condition staged; when the asset is a variant of an approved master, so the direction is already paid for; and when volume matters more than craft, provided selection time is budgeted. It is not cheaper when the asset carries the campaign idea, because direction dominates that cost.</p>
